WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.080
Every teacher has met this student.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.820
They talk a lot in class.

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.120
They sound like they know what is going on.

4
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.680
But they never did the reading.

5
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.480
We have all had one.

6
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.020
Until now, we could not see it.

7
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.160
Here is what one professor built, starting in June.

8
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.380
The first 30 lessons took about 17 weeks.

9
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.640
The next 173 took about eight days.

10
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.180
That is 203 lessons, in six courses, free for any student.

11
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.813
When you finish a goal, move up one level.

12
00:00:33.373 --> 00:00:35.693
One course became a whole pre-med curriculum.

13
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:40.413
Standard questions became questions that send a student back to the exact lesson

14
00:00:40.413 --> 00:00:41.413
that explains it.

15
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:43.017
There was a catch.

16
00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:47.697
In one course, an AI fact-checker went through nearly 3,500 claims.

17
00:00:48.337 --> 00:00:50.317
It found 49 that were flat wrong.

18
00:00:50.937 --> 00:00:53.337
It found 453 that were imprecise.

19
00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:55.117
Making lessons got cheap.

20
00:00:55.757 --> 00:00:56.937
Checking them became the job.

21
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.170
Now the classroom side.

22
00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.210
Before class, students do pre-work.

23
00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.890
They predict. They commit to an answer.

24
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.670
Then they see the result.

25
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.770
We grade the reasoning, not the correctness.

26
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.510
A wrong answer argued well costs nothing.

27
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:15.713
Then we can see who did it.

28
00:01:16.113 --> 00:01:19.293
In a class of 12 on Thursday morning, two students had finished.

29
00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:24.353
By 8:40 on Friday morning, nine had finished, and three more were partway through.

30
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:27.057
Class opens with the room's own words.

31
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:30.097
Does a dynein block prove TrkA is the cargo?

32
00:01:30.817 --> 00:01:32.877
Two say yes. Six say no.

33
00:01:33.357 --> 00:01:37.537
Nobody is named, and everyone learns in the first minute that the work was read.

34
00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:41.697
Each student also gets a short note before class on what to focus on.

35
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.370
The real risk is lazy thinking.

36
00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.430
Slow thinking is the point.

37
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.930
So we design the tool so it cannot think for the student.

38
00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.470
Reasoning is graded.

39
00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:57.230
Paste is guarded. A student who wants to skip a step writes a stuck note first, at

40
00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:01.330
least 25 words. And a human checks before anything is sent.

41
00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.090
Once the AI said a student had no activity.

42
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:05.450
That student had finished.

43
00:02:06.233 --> 00:02:10.533
If you want a place to start, there are resources for teachers at

44
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:12.013
biochemistrypedia.com/teachers.

45
00:02:12.693 --> 00:02:14.293
Fast tools. Slow thinking.
